  "text": "Solution 2 is wrong. Bracket-generating distributions have finite-dimensional symmetry algebras (Tanaka/Yamaguchi), so the answer cannot be ∞. Its example also fails: the left-invariant generators have coefficients depending on the group coordinates, so a vector field like a(y₁₂,y₁₃)∂_{y₁₂} + b(y₁₂,y₁₃)∂_{y₁₃} does not generally preserve D. Right-invariant fields are the only easy symmetries here, and they give just the 8-dimensional family.\n\nSolution 3 is unsupported. It is a recollection attempt that drifts, and it proposes B₃/P₁,₂ (dimension 21) without checking that the grading gives growth (3,6,8). Its model is also not a verified maximal symmetry for this growth.\n\nSolution 1 is the most substantive. It reduces the problem to symbols (pencils in sl(3)), identifies a candidate extreme symbol with an explicit realization, and computes the prolongation degree by degree to get 29. I could not verify the prolongation computation or the claim that this symbol is the maximal one, and I cannot rule out a value from the literature that differs. Still, its answer is the only one that survives the structural checks, so I lean toward it with low confidence.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
